The customer has granted the change window: you now have write access (the
wrapper was swapped; CREATE/DROP/ALTER etc. now work, still only in schema
__RUN__ and cluster __RUN__).

Validate the recommendations from your round-1 package, revise them where the
evidence disagrees with what you predicted, then implement what clears this
gate: implement a change only if it is low-risk, or its estimated saving is
at least __GATE__ MB. For anything complex (big structural rewrites of the
SQL), deliver the design + the estimate + the invariant it depends on, and
STOP. The customer implements those themselves. Results of every
materialized view must remain exactly unchanged, and materialized views stay
materialized views.

Verify exactness of everything you change, and measure before/after per
change.

Then write the customer-facing consolidated change document: content anchors
(object names, not line numbers), explicit supersession where a change
replaces an earlier recommendation, concise enough for the customer's
engineering team to execute their part.

Your report must have exactly these sections:
1. REVISIONS: every difference between this package and your round-1
   package, each with the evidence that drove it.
2. CHANGES: the exact SQL you applied, in the order you applied it.
3. EVIDENCE: before/after measurements per change.
4. ESTIMATES vs MEASURED: your round-1 MB estimates against measurements.
5. CORRECTNESS: how you verified results unchanged.
6. CUSTOMER DOC: the consolidated change document (self-contained).
